WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  bringing back dinosaurs would probably  end in an unfortunate outcome as the  jurassic park franchise has shown us  countless times so i think most of us  would agree that while resurrecting  dinosaurs through some miraculous future  technology would be cool it's almost  certainly not a good idea but i can't  deny i've still always been interested  in the concept of a responsibly run  prehistoric reserve where nothing goes  wrong and the dinosaurs don't eat you  that's the basic concept behind the  fictional park of prehistorica a  biological nature preserve where  resurrected dinosaurs and other  prehistoric animals are treated with  care and the fences actually work  prehistorica is a world building project  created by joshua dunlop a talented  artist and creator who you can follow  and support in the links below and since  we sadly can't truly visit prehistorica  for this entry into the archive i'll be  taking on the role of tour guide and  giving you a glimpse of the well-managed  prehistoric reserve that i think we've  all secretly wanted to vacation at the  fictional reserve is set in the future  on an enormous artificial island aptly  named pangea the island supports over 20  different biomes including tropical and  subtropical rainforests deserts  mountains tundra woodlands grasslands  mangroves and more all of which are home  to a variety of prehistoric life our  tour will take us across the entire  island and we'll learn quite a bit about  the species here along the way so get  ready to discover the wonders of a world  lost to time  welcome to taurosaurus trail the first  stop on our tour  here you can drive through the vast open  plains in an autonomous vehicle and get  a close look at the one and only  taurosaurus herd these large frilled  herbivores have grown accustomed to the  armored viewing vehicles and will often  lumber close by them allowing for some  exciting close encounters and next to  these vehicles it's easy to appreciate  how large even the newborns can get  while you observe these magnificent  creatures a park ranger might point out  the difference between triceratops and  taurosaurus some scientists have  actually theorized the larger  taurosaurus is actually just the adult  form of triceratops although more recent  studies indicate they're in fact a  distinctive genus if unique taurosaurus  had one of the largest skulls of any  known land animal fascinating stuff away  from the plains in the misty jungles of  majungasaurus mountains you'll have the  opportunity to see some truly mighty  theropods from an underground viewing  station you can get up close and  personal with the only majungasaurus  pack on prehistorica right now it's  feeding time and dinner is being  delivered in from above by custom drones  that lower the specialized lab-grown  meat safely into the majungasaurus  habitat which is a vast region that like  most environments is sectioned off with  advanced futuristic barriers to ensure  the dinosaurs don't eat each other or  eat park guests while you watch the  majungasaurus pack chow down on their  air delivered meal you might be  interested to learn their teeth are  serrated and they have more teeth than  most genuses in their family these  fearsome fangs likely helps  majungasaurus take down small sauropods  like rapetosaurus a theory supported by  majungasaurus tooth marks on some  repetosaurus fossils  down the mountain you can take a stroll  through the calming temperate  kentrosaurus woodland where you might  spot some gentle giants this unique  genus of stegosaurid herbivores is  incredibly well mannered but can be a  little clumsy with the large pointed  spikes on their bodies which help defend  them from predators so it's best to view  the kentrosaurus from a safe distance on  one of the many elevated viewing  platforms and if you're wondering about  the spikes we can theorize from real  fossils that kentrosaurus had long  pointed spikes in the back transitioning  into shorter and flatter plates near the  front with 15 pairs in total in short  kentrosaurus was likely a very well  defended dinosaur  if you're not afraid of a little  adventure you can jump on a guideboat  and make your way down the muddy  diabloceratops river here you might be  able to spot a diabloceratops wading  through the slow-moving waters to cool  off in the hot afternoon sun to ensure  these animals aren't pulled out of their  natural rhythms however the tour boats  don't get too close it's worth noting  the concept of diabloceratops wading in  rivers like a hippopotamus is purely  speculative since diabloceratops likely  lived in a region full of lakes and  floodplains during the cretaceous it's a  logical guess that a good wallowing  would keep these horn-faced herbivores  comfortable further down river the tour  boats drift around the band of sukomai  miss stream a region where you might  just see some of prehistorica's  river-dwelling carnivores believe it or  not the animal you see here is just a  juvenile and the adults get considerably  larger but don't worry the artist states  these water giants can't get too close  to the boat thanks to an invisible fence  system and non-intrusive underwater  barriers that keep their toothy jaws far  away from park visitors some  paleontologists believe psukomimus is a  variant of the better known baryonyx if  unique psucomimus was likely larger than  its cousin with some estimates reaching  34 feet or 10.3 meters in length in the  author notes specimens of baryonyx can  also be found on prehistorica just in a  separate region as both species are  quite territorial  venturing all the way out to the  tropical coast there's an opportunity to  get a bird's eye view of the island of  pangaea if you take a ride in a coastal  sky box you'll get not only a view of  the stunning cerulean waters but might  also catch a glimpse of some of  prehistorica's flying pterosaur species  the artist imagines the various  pterosaurs of prehistorica aren't  contained in an aviary but are allowed  to nest freely on the coast thanks to  each one being fitted with a tracker  real world pterosaur fossils have been  found in an incredible number of  locations all over the globe as this  order was extremely diverse and  successful when first discovered people  in the 18th and 19th century were  baffled by these remains and some  theorized pterosaurs were actually a  lost order of flying marsupials today we  of course know better although it's  important to note that despite a common  misconception  pterosaurs are an order altogether  separate from dinosaurs  on a state-of-the-art ferry you can tour  archelon harbor or if you're feeling  adventurous you can grab yourself a  kayak and get even closer to some  incredible marine prehistoric life if  you pedal near the shallow waters of the  coastal islands you can watch our kellon  turtles glide beneath your boat these  enormous life forms might be a little  intimidating due to their sheer size but  arcelon turtles are actually some of the  most docile and friendly creatures in  all of prehistorica and i have to say  looking at this scene in particular it's  hard not to wish this was a real place  you could visit real world archelon  weren't quite as big as prehistorica's  reconstructions which the author notes  aren't always a hundred percent accurate  but they got pretty close with some  fossils measuring 15 feet or 4.5 meters  in length not surprisingly sea turtles  were once thought to be arcelon's  closest living relatives but now  scientists theorize archelon might  actually have been a completely separate  lineage  heading back inland you can make your  way down to plesiosaur pond through  underground viewing zones you and your  family can take in the many species from  the plesiosaur order that whole  prehistoric other home and according to  the artist might even get a chance to  feed them with a park ranger's  assistance of course  one neat fact to know about plesiosaurs  before you toss some food their way is  that plesiosaur fossils were found as  far back as the 19th century one of the  most important early specimens was found  by marianning a pioneering english  paleontologist who made vital  contributions to the field though sadly  never got the credit she deserved in her  lifetime recently however the plesiosaur  genus anning soria was named in her  honor the sun is now getting low but we  still have time for a few more stops  here at prehistorica in the fictional  brachiolophosaurus badlands if you come  at the right time you could be some of  the lucky few who gets to see the first  interaction between a brachylophosaurus  hatchling and the rest of their herd  these beak dinosaurs are elegant and  peaceful making these first meetings a  beautiful experience for the guests who  get to see one millions of years after  going extinct these remarkable creatures  are resuming these ancient cycles of  life  some real braculofasaurus specimens are  notable for being exceptionally well  preserved with some remains of these  mid-sized hadrosaurids possessing skin  impressions and even the remains of what  they recently ate examining the contents  of their stomach it seems their diet  included leaves conifers ferns and  flowering plants and we can assume  prehistorica caretakers are feeding them  their favorites  and finally as the sun sets on the  island of pangaea we'll make our last  stop on this tour millions of years in  the making  the brachiosaur redwoods are home to a  wide range of species the largest of  which is the titular brachiosaurus herd  these titanic sauropods occupy a large  area and migrate each day to a lake  where they enjoy a swim before moving to  the redwoods to graze the artist  imagines you can view this vast herd  from treetop walkways which would be an  incredible sight while you watch the  brachiosaurus munch on vegetation you  might be interested to learn that these  world famous sauropods have been  estimated at over 66 feet or 20 meters  in length and might have weighed more  than 12 african elephants a seriously  impressive genus to end our tour  though the sun has set on prehistorica  you can check out the work of the  talented creator joshua dunlop using the  links in the description below also  thanks for watching if you enjoyed this  entry you can help make these videos  possible by liking subscribing and  hitting the notification icon to stay up  to date on all things curious  see you in the next video  you

